WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.827
Authentication and authorization are
related, but they answer different questions.

2
00:00:03.907 --> 00:00:06.322
Authentication identifies a user, service,
job, or workload.

3
00:00:06.402 --> 00:00:08.316
Authorization decides what that identity
may do.

4
00:00:08.396 --> 00:00:10.629
In a Go service, authentication may happen
in middleware,

5
00:00:10.709 --> 00:00:13.762
but permission checks still need to be
close enough to the sensitive action that

6
00:00:13.842 --> 00:00:15.482
reviewers can see what is being protected.

7
00:00:15.562 --> 00:00:17.932
Middleware can attach trusted identity
context to a request,

8
00:00:18.012 --> 00:00:21.293
but route handlers and service functions
should not assume that every authenticated

9
00:00:21.373 --> 00:00:22.512
caller has every permission.

10
00:00:22.592 --> 00:00:25.144
A route might need to check a role, a
permission, a tenant boundary,

11
00:00:25.224 --> 00:00:27.822
an object owner, a workflow state, or a
service-to-service policy.

12
00:00:27.902 --> 00:00:31.183
Missing or unclear authorization context
should fail closed rather than fall back to

13
00:00:31.263 --> 00:00:31.809
broad access.

14
00:00:31.889 --> 00:00:35.216
Session-aware code should avoid trusting
client-controlled fields as enforcement.

15
00:00:35.296 --> 00:00:37.757
A request may include user IDs, tenant
IDs, or role-like values,

16
00:00:37.837 --> 00:00:40.890
but trusted authorization context should
come from server-side session data,

17
00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:42.747
validated tokens, or authoritative
records.

18
00:00:42.827 --> 00:00:45.698
User interface behavior can guide users,
but it is not a security boundary.

19
00:00:45.778 --> 00:00:48.558
Testing should include unauthorized cases,
not only successful calls.

20
00:00:48.638 --> 00:00:51.736
Include missing sessions, expired
sessions, wrong tenant, wrong object owner,

21
00:00:51.816 --> 00:00:53.776
and service identities with limited
permissions.

22
00:00:53.856 --> 00:00:56.727
Consistent authorization helpers can make
this testing easier and reduce

23
00:00:56.807 --> 00:00:57.718
route-by-route drift.

24
00:00:57.798 --> 00:01:00.806
A secure Go application treats access
control as explicit application logic,

25
00:01:00.886 --> 00:01:02.390
not an assumption hidden in the client.

26
00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:04.748
Authorization should also be readable
during code review.

27
00:01:04.828 --> 00:01:07.198
A reviewer should be able to identify the
protected resource,

28
00:01:07.278 --> 00:01:09.101
the caller identity, the required
permission,

29
00:01:09.181 --> 00:01:12.370
and the decision point without tracing
through unrelated formatting or transport

30
00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:12.678
code.

31
00:01:12.758 --> 00:01:15.675
When those pieces are visible, it is
easier to notice missing tenant checks,

32
00:01:15.755 --> 00:01:18.580
broad administrative paths, or behavior
that changed during a refactor.
